    96	
    97	\begin{abstract}
    98	We study the existence of (unmixed) Beauville structures in finite $p$-groups, where $p$ is a prime.
    99	First of all, we extend Catanese's characterisation of abelian Beauville groups to finite $p$-groups satisfying certain conditions which are much weaker than commutativity.
   100	This result applies to all known families of $p$-groups with a good behaviour with respect to powers: regular $p$-groups, powerful $p$-groups and more generally potent $p$-groups, and (generalised) $p$-central $p$-groups.
   101	In particular, our characterisation holds for all $p$-groups of order at most $p^{\hspace{.5pt}p}$, which allows us to determine the exact number of Beauville groups of order $p^5$, for $p\ge 5$, and of order $p^6$, for $p\ge 7$.
   102	On the other hand, we determine which quotients of the Nottingham group over $\F_p$ are Beauville groups, for an odd prime $p$.
   103	As a consequence, we give the first explicit infinite family of Beauville $3$-groups, and we show that there are Beauville $3$-groups of order $3^n$ for every
   104	$n\ge 5$.
   105	\end{abstract}
   106	
   107	
   108	
   109	\maketitle
   110	
   111	
   112	
   113	
   114	\section{Introduction}
   115	
   116	
   117	
   118	A \emph{Beauville surface\/} (of unmixed type) is a compact complex surface isomorphic to a quotient
   119	$(C_1\times C_2)/G$, where $C_1$ and $C_2$ are algebraic curves of genera at least $2$ and $G$ is a finite group acting freely on $C_1\times C_2$ by holomorphic transformations, in such a way that $C_i/G\cong \P_1(\C)$ and the covering map $C_i\rightarrow C_i/G$ is ramified over three points for $i=1,2$.
   120	Then the group $G$ is said to be a \emph{Beauville group\/}.
   121	
   122	
   123	
   124	The question as to which finite groups are Beauville groups has received considerable attention in recent times.
   125	It can be reformulated in purely group-theoretical terms as follows.
   126	Given two elements $x$ and $y$ of a group $G$, we define
   127	\[
   128	\Sigma(x,y)
   129	=
   130	\bigcup_{g\in G} \,
   131	\Big( \langle x \rangle^g \cup \langle y \rangle^g \cup \langle xy \rangle^g \Big),
   132	\]
   133	that is, the union of the subgroups that belong to the conjugacy classes of $\langle x \rangle$,
   134	$\langle y \rangle$ and $\langle xy \rangle$.
   135	An (unmixed) \emph{Beauville structure\/} for $G$ is then a pair of generating sets $\{x_1,y_1\}$
   136	and $\{x_2,y_2\}$ of $G$ such that $\Sigma(x_1,y_1) \cap \Sigma(x_2,y_2)=1$, and $G$ is a Beauville group if and only if $G$ possesses a Beauville structure.
   137	In particular, Beauville groups are $2$-generator groups.
   138	
   139	
   140	
   141	Research activity around Beauville groups has been very intense since the beginning of this century; see, for example, the recent survey papers
   142	\cite{bos,fai,jon}.
   143	We briefly mention some results that we want to highlight.
   144	In 2000, Catanese \cite{cat} proved that a finite abelian group is a Beauville group if and only if it is isomorphic to $C_n\times C_n$, where  $\gcd(n,6)=1$.
   145	In 2006, Bauer, Catanese, and Grunewald \cite[Conjecture 7.17]{BCG} conjectured that every finite non-abelian simple group other than $A_5$ is a Beauville group.
   146	By using probabilistic methods, Garion, Larsen, and Lubotzky \cite{GLL} showed in 2012 that the conjecture is true if the order of the group is large enough.
   147	Soon afterwards, Guralnick and Malle \cite{GM} gave a complete proof of the conjecture, and Fairbairn, Magaard and Parker \cite{FMP,FMPc}
   148	proved that actually all finite quasisimple groups are Beauville, with the only exceptions of $A_5$ and $SL_2(5)$.
   149	
   150	
   151	
   152	At the other side of the spectrum, if $p$ is a prime, the knowledge about which finite $p$-groups are Beauville groups is very scarce, and is restricted to either groups of small order or with a very simple structure.
   153	Barker, Boston, and Fairbairn \cite{BBF} have determined all Beauville $p$-groups of order at most $p^4$, and have found estimates for the number of Beauville groups of orders $p^5$ and $p^6$.
   154	They have also proved the existence of a non-abelian Beauville $p$-group of order $p^n$ for every $p\ge 5$ and every $n\ge 3$, and have shown that the smallest non-abelian Beauville $p$-groups for $p=2$ and $p=3$ are of order $2^7$ and $3^5$, respectively.
   155	Some of these results from \cite{BBF} rely on computations with the computer algebra system MAGMA.
   156	On the other hand, as a consequence of the main theorem in \cite{BBPV}, there are Beauville $2$-groups of arbitrarily high order.
   157	Finally, let us mention that Stix and Vdovina have shown in \cite[Theorem 3]{SV} that a split metacyclic
   158	$p$-group $G$ is a Beauville group if and only if $p\ge 5$ and $G$ is a semidirect product of two cyclic groups of the same order.
   159	
   160	
   161	
   162	Our goal in this paper is to deepen the knowledge about Beauville $p$-groups.
   163	Notice that Catanese's characterisation of abelian Beauville groups implies that a $2$-generator abelian $p$-group of exponent $p^e$ is a Beauville group if and only if $p\ge 5$ and
   164	$|G^{p^{e-1}}|=p^2$.
   165	In Section 2, we provide a generalisation of this result to a wide class of non-abelian $p$-groups.
   166	
   167	
   168	
   169	\begin{thmA}
   170	Let $G$ be a $2$-generator finite $p$-group of exponent $p^e$, and suppose that $G$ satisfies one of the following conditions:
   171	\begin{enumerate}
   172	\item
   173	For $x,y\in G$, we have $x^{p^{e-1}} = y^{p^{e-1}}$ if and only if $(xy^{-1})^{p^{e-1}} = 1$.
   174	\item
   175	$G$ is a potent $p$-group.
   176	\end{enumerate}
   177	Then $G$ is a Beauville group if and only if $p\ge 5$ and $|G^{p^{e-1}}|\ge p^2$.
   178	\end{thmA}
   179	
   180	
   181	
   182	Recall that a finite $p$-group is potent if either $p>2$ and $\gamma_{p-1}(G)\le G^p$, or $p=2$ and $G'\le G^4$.
   183	They encompass the well-known powerful $p$-groups.
   184	Actually, Theorem A applies to all usual families of $p$-groups with a `nice power structure'.
   185	Observe that (i) is known to hold for regular $p$-groups and for $p$-central $p$-groups.
   186	More generally, we prove that (i) holds for the $p$-groups that we call generalised $p$-central.
   187	These are defined by conditions which are dual to those for potent $p$-groups, namely that $\Omega_1(G)\le Z_{p-2}(G)$ if $p$ is odd and that $\Omega_2(G)\le Z(G)$ if $p=2$.
   188	
   189	Since $p$-groups of class less than $p$ are regular, they are also covered by Theorem A.
   190	Thus we can use it to determine in a rather straightforward way whether any given group of order at most $p^{\hspace{0.5pt}p}$ is a Beauville group.
   191	In particular, we determine all Beauville $p$-groups of order $p^5$, for $p\ge 5$, and of order $p^6$, for $p\ge 7$, completing the work in \cite{BBF}.
   192	Theorem A can be similarly applied to obtain all Beauville groups of order $p^7$ for $p\ge 7$, but we have not pursued this task so far.
   193	On the other hand,  since $p$-groups in which $G'$ is cyclic are regular for odd $p$, we can extend the characterization of split metacyclic $p$-groups given in \cite{SV} to all metacyclic $p$-groups.
   194	
   195	Unlike in the abelian case, under conditions (i) or (ii) in Theorem A, it may well happen that $|G^{p^{e-1}}|>p^2$; for example, if $G$ is of exponent $p$.
   196	On the other hand, we want to remark that Theorem A is not valid for all finite $p$-groups.
   197	As a matter of fact, we will show that, for each of the implications in Theorem A, there exist infinitely many $p$-groups for which that implication fails.
   198	Even more, we will see that no condition on the order of $G^{p^{e-1}}$ can ensure the existence of Beauville structures in the class of all finite $p$-groups.
   199	
   200	
   201	
   202	\vspace{5pt}
   203	
   204	
   205	
   206	The second main result in our paper deals with the Nottingham group $\No$ over the field $\F_p$, for odd $p$.
   207	This is the group of normalised automorphisms of the ring $\F_p[[t]]$ of formal power series; every $f\in\No$ is completely determined by its image on $t$, which is of the form $f(t)=t+\sum_{i\ge 2} \, a_it^i$.
   208	The group $\No$ is a pro-$p$ group that can be topologically generated by two elements, and so every finite quotient of $\No$ is a candidate to be a Beauville group.
   209	The lattice of normal subgroups of $\No$ is well known.
   210	As a matter of fact, $\No$ is just infinite as an abstract group, i.e.\ every non-trivial normal subgroup of $\No$ is of finite index.
   211	In Section \ref{nott}, for every $1\ne \WW \trianglelefteq \No$ we are able to determine whether the factor group $\No/\WW$ is a Beauville group or not.
   212	It turns out that most quotients of $\No$, but not all, are Beauville groups, and we get the following result.
   213	
   214	
   215	
   216	\begin{thmB}
   217	Let $\No$ be the Nottingham group over $\F_p$, where $p$ is an odd prime, and let
   218	$n_0=2$ or $5$, according as $p>3$ or $p=3$.
   219	Then for every $n\ge n_0$ there exists a quotient of $\No$ of order $p^n$ which is a Beauville group.
   220	\end{thmB}
   221	
   222	
   223	
   224	The $3$-groups in Theorem B constitute the first explicit example which is known of an infinite family of Beauville $3$-groups.
   225	We observe that the existence of infinitely many Beauville $3$-groups follows from either Theorem 2 in \cite{SV} or Theorem 37 in \cite{GJ}, but these results do not provide explicit groups.
   226	
   227	
   228	
   229	\vspace{10pt}
   230	
   231	
   232	
   233	\noindent
   234	\textit{Notation.\/}
   235	If $G$ is a finite $p$-group and $i\ge 0$, we write $\Omega_{\{i\}}(G)$ for the set of all elements of $G$ of order at most $p^i$, and
   236	$\Omega_i(G)$ for the subgroup they generate.
   237	Also, $G^{p^i}$ is the subgroup generated by all powers $g^{p^i}$, as $g$ runs over $G$.
   238	The exponent of $G$, denoted by $\exp G$, is the maximum of the orders of all elements of $G$.
   239	If $g\in G$ is an element of order $p$ and $\lambda\in\F_p$, then $g^{\lambda}$ is understood to be $g^n$, where $n$ is any integer which reduces to $\lambda$ modulo $p$.
   240	The rest of the notation is standard in group theory.
   241	
   242	
   243	
   244	\section{Finite $p$-groups with a nice power structure}
   245	
   246	
   247	
   248	Our goal in this section is to prove Theorem A in the introduction, which generalises Catanese's criterion for abelian Beauville groups to all classes of finite
   249	$p$-groups with a `nice power structure' that have been considered in the literature.
   250	Then we give a number of applications of this result, like the determination of all Beauville groups of order $p^5$ for $p\ge 5$ or of order $p^6$ for $p\ge 7$, the characterisation of Beauville metacyclic $p$-groups, and also that of Beauville $p$-groups of maximal class of order at most $p^{\hspace{0.4pt}p}$.
   251	
   252	
   253	
   254	We begin by introducing a property which is essential to our result, and by studying which of the best known families of finite $p$-groups satisfy this property.
   255	Let $G$ be a finite $p$-group, and let $i\ge 1$ be an integer.
   256	Following Xu \cite{xu}, we say that $G$ is \emph{semi-$p^i$-abelian\/} if the following condition holds:
   257	\begin{equation}
   258	\label{semi-pi-abelian}
   259	x^{p^i} = y^{p^i}
   260	\quad
   261	\text{if and only if}
   262	\quad
   263	(xy^{-1})^{p^i} = 1.
   264	\end{equation}
   265	If $G$ is semi-$p^i$-abelian, then we have \cite[Lemma 1]{xu}:
   266	\begin{enumerate}
   267	\item[(SA1)]
   268	$\Omega_i(G)=\{x\in G\mid x^{p^i}=1\}$.
   269	\item[(SA2)]
   270	The cardinality of the set $\{x^{p^i}\mid x\in G\}$ coincides with the index $|G:\Omega_i(G)|$.
   271	\end{enumerate}
   272	If $G$ is semi-$p^i$-abelian for every $i\ge 1$, then $G$ is called \emph{strongly semi-$p$-abelian\/}.
   273	
   274	For example, regular $p$-groups are strongly semi-$p$-abelian \cite[Theorem 3.14]{suz2}.
   275	On the other hand, a powerful $p$-group of exponent $p^e$ is semi-$p^{e-1}$-abelian, as follows from Lemma 3 of \cite{fer}.
   276	Also, Xu proved that, for odd $p$, any finite $p$-group satisfying $\Omega_1(\gamma_{p-1}(G))\le Z(G)$ is strongly semi-$p$-abelian.
   277	This applies in particular to the case $\Omega_1(G)\le Z(G)$, i.e.\ to $p$-central $p$-groups.
   278	Next we consider two other families of $p$-groups, which are generalisations of $p$-central $p$-groups and powerful $p$-groups, respectively, and we study their behaviour with respect to property (\ref{semi-pi-abelian}).
   279	
   280	
   281	
   282	\begin{dfn}
   283	Let $G$ be a finite $p$-group.
   284	Then:
   285	\begin{enumerate}
   286	\item
   287	$G$ is \emph{generalised $p$-central\/} if $\Omega_1(G)\le Z_{p-2}(G)$ for odd $p$, and if
   288	$\Omega_2(G)\le Z(G)$ if $p=2$.
   289	\item
   290	$G$ is \emph{potent\/} if $\gamma_{p-1}(G)\le G^p$ for odd $p$, and if $G'\le G^4$ for $p=2$. 
   291	\end{enumerate}
   292	\end{dfn}
   293	
   294	
   295	
   296	Observe the duality between the two cases.
   297	Clearly, when $p=2$ or $3$ these concepts coincide with $p$-central $p$-groups and with powerful $p$-groups.
   298	Generalised $p$-central $p$-groups have been studied by Gonz\'alez-S\'anchez and Weigel \cite{GW}, and potent $p$-groups by Arganbright \cite{arg}, Lubotzky and Mann \cite{LM}, and Gonz\'alez-S\'anchez and Jaikin-Zapirain \cite{GJ2}.
   299	If $G$ is generalised $p$-central then, by \cite[Theorem B]{GW}, we have
   300	\[
   301	\Omega_i(G) = \{ g\in G \mid g^{p^i}=1 \},
   302	\quad
   303	\text{for all $i\ge 1$,}
   304	\]
   305	and if $G$ is potent then, by \cite[Theorem 2]{arg} and \cite[Proposition 1.7]{LM}, we have
   306	\[
   307	G^{p^i} = \{ g^{p^i} \mid g\in G \},
   308	\quad
   309	\text{for all $i\ge 1$.}
   310	\]
   311	
   312	
   313	
   314	\begin{thm}
   315	A generalised $p$-central $p$-group is strongly semi-$p$-abelian.
   316	\end{thm}
   317	
   318	
   319	
   320	\begin{proof}
   321	Given $x,y\in G$, we prove that $x^{p^i}=y^{p^i}$ if and only if $(xy^{-1})^{p^i}=1$ by induction on $i\ge 1$.
   322	We first deal with the case $i=1$, by induction on the order of $G$.
   323	Thus the result holds in any proper subgroup of $G$, and we may assume that $G=\langle x,y \rangle$.
   324	
   325	Let us see that $x^p=y^p$ implies $(xy^{-1})^p=1$.
   326	We have $(x^y)^p=(x^p)^y=x^p$.
   327	Since $\langle x,x^y \rangle<G$ unless $G$ is cyclic, it follows that $[x,y]^p=(x^{-1}x^y)^p=1$.
   328	Consequently $G'\le \Omega_1(G)$.
   329	If $p$ is odd, then $G'\le Z_{p-2}(G)$, and $\gamma_p(G)=[G',G,\overset{p-2}{\ldots},G]=1$.
   330	Thus $G$ is regular and $(xy^{-1})^p=1$, as desired.
   331	If $p=2$ then, since $x^2=y^2$, we have $G/G'=C/G'\times D/G'$ with $C/G'$ and $D/G'$ cyclic, and $D/G'\cong C_2$.
   332	Then $D\le \Omega_2(G)\le Z(G)$ and $G/Z(G)$ is cyclic.
   333	Thus $G$ is abelian and the result is valid also in this case.
   334	
   335	Now we prove that $(xy^{-1})^p=1$ implies $x^p=y^p$.
   336	We have $(xy^{-1})^p=1=(y^{-1}x)^p$ and, by the previous paragraph, $[x,y^{-1}]^p=1$.
   337	Consequently again $G'\le\Omega_1(G)$ and then, as above, $G$ is regular (i.e.\ abelian if $p=2$) and the result holds.
   338	
   339	Finally, we consider the case $i>1$.
   340	As shown above, we have $x^{p^i}=y^{p^i}$ if and only if $(x^{p^{i-1}}y^{-p^{i-1}})^p=1$, which is in turn equivalent to
   341	$x^{p^{i-1}} \Omega_1(G) = y^{p^{i-1}} \Omega_1(G)$.
   342	Now, $G/\Omega_1(G)$ is again a generalised $p$-central $p$-group, by  \cite[Theorem B]{GW}.
   343	By the induction hypothesis, the last equality is tantamount to $(xy^{-1})^{p^{i-1}}\in \Omega_1(G)$, and this means exactly that $(xy^{-1})^{p^i}=1$.
   344	\end{proof}
   345	
   346	
   347	
   348	However, potent $p$-groups are not in general strongly semi-$p$-abelian, even not semi-$p^{e-1}$-abelian as powerful $p$-groups, given that $\exp G=p^e$.
   349	
   350	
   351	
   352	\begin{exa}
   353	Let $p>3$ be a prime and let
   354	\[
   355	A
   356	=
   357	\langle a_1\rangle \times \dots\times\langle a_p\rangle
   358	\cong
   359	C_p\times\overset{p-2}{\dots}\times C_p\times C_{p^2}\times C_{p^2}.
   360	\]
   361	We define an automorphism $\alpha$ of $A$ by means of $\alpha(a_i)=a_ia_{i+1}$, for $i=1,\ldots,p-3$, $\alpha(a_{p-2})=a_{p-2}a_{p-1}^p$,
   362	$\alpha(a_{p-1})=a_{p-1}a_p$, and $\alpha(a_p)=a_p$.
   363	Then $\alpha$ is of order $p^2$, and we can construct a semidirect product $G=\langle b\rangle \ltimes A$, where $b$ is of order $p^2$ and acts on $A$ via
   364	$\alpha$.
   365	One readily checks that $\gamma_{p-1}(G)=\langle a_{p-1}^p, a_p^p \rangle \leq G^p$, and hence $G$ is a potent $p$-group.
   366	Also, we have $\exp G=p^2$.
   367	However, $G$ is not semi-$p$-abelian, since $x=ba_1$ and $y=ba_p$ satisfy that $x^p=b^pa_p^p=y^p$, but $(xy^{-1})^p=a_p^{-p}\ne 1$.
   368	\end{exa}
   369	
   370	
   371	
   372	After this preliminary analysis of the finite $p$-groups satisfying condition (\ref{semi-pi-abelian}), we proceed to the proof of Theorem A.
   373	We start with a result of a more general nature that can be used to prove the non-existence of Beauville structures.
   374	
   375	
   376	
   377	\begin{pro}
   378	\label{negative}
   379	Let $G$ be a $2$-generator finite $p$-group of exponent $p^e$, and suppose that:
   380	\begin{enumerate}
   381	\item
   382	$\Omega_{\{e-1\}}(G)$ is contained in the union of two maximal subgroups of $G$.
   383	\item
   384	$|G^{p^{e-1}}|=p$.
   385	\end{enumerate}
   386	Then $G$ is not a Beauville group.
   387	\end{pro}
   388	
   389	
   390	
   391	\begin{proof}
   392	We argue by way of contradiction.
   393	Suppose $\{x_1,y_1\}$ and $\{x_2,y_2\}$ are two systems of generators of $G$ such that $\Sigma(x_1,y_1)\cap \Sigma(x_2,y_2)=1$.
   394	Since no two of the elements $x_1$, $y_1$ and $x_1y_1$ can lie in the same maximal subgroup of $G$, it follows from (i) that one of these elements, say $x_1$, is of order $p^e$.
   395	Similarly, we may assume that the order of $x_2$ is also $p^e$.
   396	Since $G^{p^{e-1}}$ is of order $p$, we conclude that $\langle x_1^{p^{e-1}} \rangle=\langle x_2^{p^{e-1}} \rangle$, which is a contradiction.
   397	\end{proof}
   398	
   399	
   400	
   401	As we will see at the end of the paper, we cannot relax condition (i) in the last proposition, since there are examples of groups $G$ in which
   402	$\Omega_{\{e-1\}}(G)$ is contained in the union of three maximal subgroups, and which are Beauville groups even if $G^{p^{e-1}}$ is of order $p$.
   403	
   404	
   405	
   406	The following result is an extended version of Theorem A.
   407	
   408	
   409	
   410	\begin{thm}
   411	\label{characterisation general}
   412	Let $G$ be a $2$-generator finite $p$-group of exponent $p^e$ which satisfies one of the following conditions:
   413	\begin{enumerate}
   414	\item
   415	$G$ is semi-$p^{e-1}$-abelian, i.e.\ for every $x,y\in G$, we have $x^{p^{e-1}}=y^{p^{e-1}}$ if and only if $(xy^{-1})^{p^{e-1}}=1$.
   416	\item
   417	$G$ is potent.
   418	\end{enumerate}
   419	Then $G$ is a Beauville group if and only if $p\ge 5$ and $|G^{p^{e-1}}|\ge p^2$.
   420	If that is the case, then every lift of a Beauville structure of $G/\Phi(G)$ is a Beauville structure of $G$.
   421	\end{thm}
   422	
   423	
   424	
   425	\begin{proof}
   426	Before proceeding, notice that in any case $\Omega_{e-1}(G)$ coincides with the set of all elements of $G$ of order at most $p^{e-1}$, and the cardinality of
   427	\[
   428	X = \{ g^{p^{e-1}} \mid g\in G \}
   429	\]
   430	coincides with the index $|G:\Omega_{e-1}(G)|$.
   431	This follows from (SA1) and (SA2) if $G$ is semi-$p^{e-1}$-abelian, and from Theorem 6.6 of \cite{GJ2} if $G$ is potent.
   432	Next, we claim that the condition $|G^{p^{e-1}}|\ge p^2$ implies that $\Omega_{e-1}(G)$ is contained in $\Phi(G)$.
   433	Since $\Phi(G)=G'G^p\le G'\Omega_{e-1}(G)$, we have
   434	\begin{equation}
   435	\label{chain}
   436	|G/\Omega_{e-1}(G):(G/\Omega_{e-1}(G))'|
   437	=
   438	|G:G'\Omega_{e-1}(G)|
   439	\le
   440	|G:\Phi(G)| = p^2.
   441	\end{equation}
   442	If $|G/\Omega_{e-1}(G):(G/\Omega_{e-1}(G))'|\le p$, then the quotient $G/\Omega_{e-1}(G)$ is cyclic, and so it has order at most $p$.
   443	It follows that $|X|\le p$, and so also $|G^{p^{e-1}}|\le p$, which is a contradiction.
   444	Thus $|G/\Omega_{e-1}(G):(G/\Omega_{e-1}(G))'|\ge p^2$, and this, together with (\ref{chain}), yields that $\Omega_{e-1}(G)\le \Phi(G)$.
   445	This proves the claim and, as a consequence, all elements outside $\Phi(G)$ are of order $p^e$.
   446	 
   447	Now we show that $G$ is a Beauville group if $p\ge 5$ and $|G^{p^{e-1}}|\ge p^2$.
   448	Since $p\ge 5$, the elementary abelian group $G/\Phi(G)$ is a Beauville group.
   449	Let us see that every lift of a Beauville structure of $G/\Phi(G)$ is a Beauville structure of $G$.
   450	If we use the bar notation in $G/\Phi(G)$, it suffices to show that, given two elements
   451	$x,y\in G\smallsetminus \Phi(G)$, the condition $\langle \overline x \rangle \cap \langle \overline y \rangle=\overline 1$ implies that $\langle x \rangle \cap \langle y \rangle=1$.
   452	Suppose for a contradiction that $\langle x \rangle \cap \langle y \rangle \ne 1$, i.e.\ that
   453	$x^{p^{e-1}}=y^{ip^{e-1}}$ for some integer $i$ not divisible by $p$.
   454	
   455	Now we deal separately with the two conditions (i) and (ii).
   456	If (i) holds then $xy^{-i}\in \Omega_{e-1}(G)$, and consequently $\langle \overline x \rangle=\langle \overline y \rangle$ in $G/\Phi(G)$, which is a contradiction.
   457	On the other hand, if $G$ is potent then we consider a maximal subgroup $M$ of $G$ containing $x$.
   458	Since $\Omega_{e-1}(G)\le M$, we have
   459	\[
   460	|M:M^{p^{e-1}}| = |\Omega_{e-1}(M)| = |\Omega_{e-1}(G)| = |G:G^{p^{e-1}}|,
   461	\]
   462	by \cite[Theorem 6.6]{GJ2}.
   463	As a consequence, $|G^{p^{e-1}}:M^{p^{e-1}}|=p$.
   464	Now the quotient group $G/M^{p^{e-1}}$ is a potent group which can be generated by two elements of order at most $p^{e-1}$, namely the images of $x$ and $y$. 
   465	Again by Theorem 6.6 of \cite{GJ2}, it follows that $\exp G/M^{p^{e-1}}\le p^{e-1}$ and $G^{p^{e-1}}\le M^{p^{e-1}}$.
   466	This contradiction completes the proof of the first implication in the statement of the theorem.
   467	
   468	Let us now prove the converse.
   469	Since $\exp G=p^e$ and $\Omega_{\{e-1\}}(G)$ is a proper subgroup of $G$, it follows from
   470	Proposition \ref{negative} that we only need to prove that $G$ has no Beauville structure if $p=2$ or $3$, provided that $|G^{p^{e-1}}|\ge p^2$.
   471	As shown above, all elements of $G\smallsetminus \Phi(G)$ are of order $p^e$.
   472	Also, if $G$ is potent then $G$ is powerful and consequently semi-$p^{e-1}$-abelian.
   473	Consequently, it suffices to prove the result in case (i).
   474	We are going to show that any Beauville structure of $G$ induces, by passing to the quotient, a Beauville structure in $G/G^p$.
   475	However, if $p=2$ then $G/G^2$ is abelian of order $4$, and if $p=3$ then $G/G^3$ is of order at most $3^3$ by \cite[14.2.3]{rob}.
   476	In both cases, $G/G^p$ does not have a Beauville structure.
   477	 	
   478	So let us see that every Beauville structure of $G$ is inherited by $G/G^p$.
   479	To this end, we see that, given $x,y\in G\smallsetminus \Phi(G)$, the condition
   480	$\langle x \rangle \cap \langle y \rangle=1$ implies that
   481	$\langle \overline x \rangle \cap \langle \overline y \rangle=\overline 1$ in $G/G^p$.
   482	If this is false, then for some $i$ not divisible by $p$ we have $xy^{-i}\in G^p$, and consequently
   483	$(xy^{-i})^{p^{e-1}}=1$.
   484	By (i), we get $x^{p^{e-1}}=y^{ip^{e-1}}$.
   485	This is a contradiction, since both $x$ and $y$ are of order $p^e$ and $\langle x \rangle \cap \langle y \rangle=1$.
   486	\end{proof}
   487	
   488	
   489	
   490	\begin{cor}
   491	\label{cor:thm A for all good families}
   492	Let $G$ be a finite $p$-group, and suppose that $G$ belongs to one of the following families:
   493	\begin{enumerate}
   494	\item
   495	Regular $p$-groups, and in particular groups of order at most $p^{\hspace{0.4pt}p}$.
   496	\item
   497	Potent $p$-groups, and in particular powerful $p$-groups.
   498	\item
   499	Generalised $p$-central $p$-groups, and in particular $p$-central $p$-groups.
   500	\item
   501	For odd $p$, groups satisfying the condition $\Omega_1(\gamma_{p-1}(G))\le Z(G)$.
   502	\end{enumerate}
   503	Then $G$ is a Beauville group if and only if $p\ge 5$ and $|G^{p^{e-1}}|\ge p^2$, where $\exp G=p^e$.
   504	\end{cor}
   505	
   506	
   507	
   508	As we see in our next result, under the hypotheses of Theorem A, the condition $|G^{p^{e-1}}|\ge p^2$ is easy to detect if we know a reasonably good presentation of $G$.
   509	
   510	 
   511	 
   512	\begin{pro}
   513	\label{easy detecting}
   514	Let $G=\langle a,b \rangle$ be a finite $p$-group of exponent $p^e$ which is either semi-$p^{e-1}$-abelian or potent, where $p\ge 5$.
   515	Then $|G^{p^{e-1}}|\ge p^2$ if and only if the two subgroups $\langle a^{p^{e-1}} \rangle$ and
   516	$\langle b^{p^{e-1}} \rangle$ are different and non-trivial.
   517	\end{pro}
   518	
   519	
   520	
   521	\begin{proof}
   522	We only need to prove the `only if' part.
   523	Thus we assume that $|G^{p^{e-1}}|\ge p^2$.
   524	From the proof of Theorem \ref{characterisation general}, we have $\Omega_{e-1}(G)\le \Phi(G)$, and so both $\langle a^{p^{e-1}} \rangle$ and
   525	$\langle b^{p^{e-1}} \rangle$ are non-trivial.
   526	Now, since $p\ge 5$, there is a Beauville structure with two systems of generators $\{a\Phi(G),y_1\Phi(G)\}$ and $\{b\Phi(G),y_2\Phi(G)\}$.
   527	Again by Theorem \ref{characterisation general}, the lift given by $\{a,y_1\}$ and $\{b,y_2\}$ is a Beauville structure of $G$.
   528	Thus $\langle a \rangle \cap \langle b \rangle=1$, and consequently $\langle a^{p^{e-1}} \rangle \ne \langle b^{p^{e-1}} \rangle$.
   529	This completes the proof.
   530	\end{proof}
   531	
   532	
   533	
   534	\begin{exa}
   535	In \cite[Conjecture 25]{BBF}, the authors conjecture that the two non-isomorphic groups of order $p^5$ under the names $H_3$ and $H_4$ in that paper are Beauville for $p\ge 5$ and, as a consequence, that there are exactly $p+10$ Beauville groups of order $p^5$ for $p\ge 5$.
   536	This can be confirmed in a straightforward way by using Proposition \ref{easy detecting}.
   537	Indeed, both isomorphism types can be described in the form
   538	\begin{gather*}
   539	G = \langle a, b, c \mid a^{p^2}=b^{p^2}=c^p=[b,c]=1,\ [a,b]=c,\ [a,c]=b^{rp} \rangle,
   540	\end{gather*}
   541	where $r$ is not divisible by $p$.
   542	Observe that $G=\langle a,b \rangle$.
   543	Since $\exp G=p^2$ and $\langle a^p \rangle \ne \langle b^p \rangle$ are non-trivial, we conclude that $G$ is a Beauville group.
   544	\end{exa}
   545	
   546	
   547	
   548	For groups of class less than $p$ (so in particular for groups of order at most $p^{\hspace{0.4pt}p}$), we can further simplify the determination of whether the group is Beauville or not by using the Lazard Correspondence.
   549	Recall that the Lazard Correspondence uses the Baker-Campbell-Hausdorff formula to establish a one-to-one correspondence between finite $p$-groups of class less than $p$ and nilpotent Lie rings of $p$-power order of class less than $p$ (see \cite[Section 10.2]{khu}).
   550	The underlying set for both the group and the Lie ring is the same, and it turns out that the $n$th power of an element in the group coincides with its $n$th multiple in the Lie ring.
   551	Thus if $G=\langle a,b \rangle$, we can check the conditions in Proposition \ref{easy detecting} by working in the Lie ring instead of in the group, i.e.\ we have to check whether $\langle p^{e-1}a \rangle$ and $\langle p^{e-1}b \rangle$ are different and non-trivial.
   552	This is particularly interesting for $p$-groups of small order, since their classification relies in classifying first nilpotent Lie rings of the same order and then applying the Lazard correspondence.
   553	For example, this is the procedure followed in \cite{NOV} and \cite{OV} to determine all groups of orders $p^6$ and $p^7$ for $p\ge 7$.
   554	By using the presentations of the nilpotent Lie rings of order $p^6$ provided in \cite{vau}, we have obtained that the number of Beauville groups of order $p^6$ is
   555	\[
   556	4p+20+4\gcd(p-1,3)+\gcd(p-1,4)
   557	\]
   558	for $p\ge 7$.
   559	Since the total number of $2$-generator groups of order $p^6$ is
   560	\[
   561	10p+62+14\gcd(p-1,3)+7\gcd(p-1,4)+2\gcd(p-1,5),
   562	\]
   563	it follows that the ratio between the number of Beauville groups and the number of all $2$-generator groups of order $p^6$ tends to $2/5$ as $p\to\infty$.
   564	Note that in \cite{BBF}, the authors could only say that this limit is smaller than $1$, by finding $p-1$ non-Beauville $2$-generator groups of order $p^6$.
   565	
   566	
   567	
   568	As an illustration of our method, let us consider the following two nilpotent Lie rings of order $p^6$ taken from
   569	\cite{vau}:
   570	\[
   571	L_1 = \langle a,b \mid p^2a, pb-[b,a,a], \text{$p$-class 3} \rangle
   572	\]
   573	and
   574	\begin{multline*}
   575	L_2 = \langle a,b \mid pa-[b,a,a,a], pb-[b,a,a,a], [b,a,a,b],
   576	\\
   577	[b,a,b,b]+[b,a,a,a], \text{$p$-class 4} \rangle.
   578	\end{multline*}
   579	Clearly, in both $L_1$ and $L_2$, the elements $a$ and $b$ are of order $p^2$, which is the exponent of the additive group of the Lie ring.
   580	In the first case, $\langle pa \rangle$ and $\langle pb \rangle$ are different subgroups, while in the second case they are equal.
   581	Thus $L_1$ gives rise to a Beauville group under the Lazard correspondence, while $L_2$ does not.
   582	
   583	It would be equally possible to calculate the exact number of Beauville groups of order $p^7$, for $p\ge 7$, but we have not pursued that task.
   584	
   585	
   586	
   587	\vspace{10pt}
   588	
   589	
   590	
   591	As a final application of Theorem A, we extend the characterisation given by Stix and Vdovina \cite[Theorem 3]{SV} of split metacyclic Beauville $p$-groups to all metacyclic $p$-groups.
   592	Our proof is self-contained and does not rely on the result of \cite{SV}.
   593	
   594	
   595	
   596	\begin{cor}
   597	A metacyclic $p$-group $G$ is a Beauville group if and only if $p\ge 5$ and $G$ is a semidirect product of two cyclic groups of the same order.
   598	\end{cor}
   599	
   600	
   601	
   602	\begin{proof}
   603	Let $G=\langle a,b \rangle$ with $\langle a \rangle \trianglelefteq G$.
   604	Assume first that $p$ is odd, and let $\exp G=p^e$.
   605	Then $G'$ is cyclic and $G$ is regular by \cite[3.13]{suz2}.
   606	Thus $G$ is semi-$p^{e-1}$-abelian and then, by Proposition \ref{easy detecting}, $G$ is a Beauville group if and only if $p\ge 5$ and the subgroups $\langle a^{p^{e-1}} \rangle$ and $\langle b^{p^{e-1}} \rangle$ are non-trivial and different.
   607	This means that $G=\langle b \rangle \ltimes \langle a \rangle$ is a semidirect product with $a$ and $b$ of the same order.
   608	
   609	Now we consider the case $p=2$.
   610	We have to prove that $G$ is not a Beauville group. 
   611	If $G'\le G^4$ then $G$ is powerful, and the result follows from Corollary \ref{cor:thm A for all good families}.
   612	Thus we assume that $G'$ is not contained in $G^4$, i.e.\ that $G'=\langle a^2 \rangle$.
   613	We claim that, for every set $\{x,y\}$ of generators, we have $bG'\subseteq \Sigma(x,y)$.
   614	This proves that $G$ is not a Beauville group also in this case.
   615	Since $G$ has only three maximal subgroups, we may assume that $x\in \langle b \rangle G^2 \smallsetminus G^2$.
   616	Since $G^2=\langle b^2 \rangle G'$, we can write $x=b^iw$ with $i$ odd and $w\in G'$.
   617	Then there is a power of $x$ of the form $x^*=bw^*$, for some $w^*\in G'$.
   618	Now observe that
   619	\[
   620	G' = \langle [x^*,a] \rangle = \{ [x^*,a]^i \mid i\in\N \} = \{ [x^*,a^i] \mid i\in\N \},
   621	\]
   622	and consequently the conjugacy class of $x^*$ equals $x^*G'=bG'$.
   623	This proves that $bG'$ is contained in $\Sigma(x,y)$, as desired.
   624	\end{proof}
   625	
   626	
   627	
   628	\begin{cor}
   629	Let $G$ be $p$-group of maximal class of order at most $p^{\hspace{0.4pt}p}$.
   630	Then $G$ is a Beauville group if and only if $p\ge 5$ and $\exp G=p$.
   631	\end{cor}
   632	
   633	
   634	
   635	\begin{proof}
   636	By a result of Blackburn (see \cite[Theorem 3.2]{bla} or \cite[Lemma 14.14]{hup}), we have $\exp G/Z(G)=p$.
   637	Thus $|G^p|\le p$ and $\exp G=p$ or $p^2$.
   638	By Theorem A, $G$ is a Beauville group if and only if $p\ge 5$ and $\exp G=p$.
   639	\end{proof}
   640	
   641	
   642	
   643	The assumptions (i) or (ii) are essential in Theorem \ref{characterisation general}.
   644	Indeed, for a general finite $p$-group $G$, the condition that $|G^{p^{e-1}}|\ge p^2$ is neither sufficient nor necessary for $G$ to be a Beauville group.
   645	We show this in Corollary \ref{cor:both implications fail}, by using quotients of some infinite pro-$p$ groups that we define now.
   646	
   647	
   648	
   649	Let $k\ge 1$ be a fixed integer, and consider the ring of integers $R$ of the cyclotomic field $\Q_p(\zeta)$, where $\Q_p$ is the field of $p$-adic numbers and
   650	$\zeta$ is a primitive $p^k$th root of unity.
   651	Then $R=\Z_p[\zeta]$ is a discrete valuation ring and a free $\Z_p$-module of rank $p^{k-1}(p-1)$.
   652	Also, the element $\zeta-1$ is a uniformizer, and we have
   653	\begin{equation}
   654	\label{eqn:p ideal}
   655	(p)=(\zeta-1)^{p^{k-1}(p-1)}.
   656	\end{equation}
   657	Multiplication by $\zeta$ defines an automorphism of order $p^k$ of the additive group of $R$, which can be used to construct a split extension of $R$ by $C_{p^k}$.
   658	In order to avoid mixing additive and multiplicative notation, we consider a multiplicative copy $A$ of $R$, via an isomorphism $\varphi:A\rightarrow R$.
   659	If $C=\langle t \rangle$ is a cyclic group of order $p^k$, then we define $P_k=C\ltimes A$, where the action of $t$ on $A$ corresponds under $\varphi$ to multiplication by $\zeta$, that is, $\varphi(a^t)=\zeta\varphi(a)$ for all $a\in A$.
   660	Observe that $P_k$ is a $2$-generator pro-$p$ group, topologically generated by $t$ and by $a_1=\varphi^{-1}(1)$.
   661	Also, we have $P_k'=[A,t]$, which corresponds to the ideal $(\zeta-1)$ of $R$ under $\varphi$.
   662	More generally, the lower central series of $P_k$ consists of the subgroups $[A,t,\ldots,t]$, and the action of $C$ on $A$ is uniserial.
   663	In particular, if $k=1$ then we get the only infinite pro-$p$ group of maximal class.
   664	
   665	
   666	
   667	As we next see, the pro-$p$ groups $P_k$ are a source of infinitely many Beauville $p$-groups.
   668	
   669	
   670	
   671	\begin{thm}
   672	\label{thm:quotients of Pk}
   673	Let $p\ge 5$ be a prime and let $k\ge 1$ be an integer.
   674	If $N$ is a normal subgroup of $P_k$ of finite index and $N\le A^p$, then the factor group $P_k/N$ is a Beauville group.
   675	\end{thm}
   676	
   677	
   678	
   679	\begin{proof}
   680	By \cite[Theorem 1.17]{DDSMS}, $N$ is open in $P_k$, and consequently, $P_k/N$ is a $2$-generator $p$-group.
   681	For every $a\in A$, we have
   682	\begin{equation}
   683	\label{eqn:power of ta}
   684	(ta)^{p^k} = t^{p^k} a^{\sum_{i=0}^{p^k-1} \, t^i}.
   685	\end{equation}
   686	Since $\zeta$ is a primitive $p^k$th root of unity and $t^{p^k}=1$, it follows that $(ta)^{p^k}=1$.
   687	Now the image of $ta$ in $P_k/A$ is of order $p^k$, and consequently $taN$ is of order $p^k$ in $P_k/N$ as well.
   688	
   689	Let $a,b\in A$ and assume that the subgroups generated by $taN$ and $tbN$ have non-trivial intersection.
   690	Then these subgroups have the same $p^{k-1}$st power, and it readily follows that
   691	$(taN)^{p^{k-1}}=(tbN)^{p^{k-1}}$.
   692	By a calculation similar to (\ref{eqn:power of ta}), we get
   693	\[
   694	a^{\sum_{i=0}^{p^{k-1}-1} \, t^i} \equiv b^{\sum_{i=0}^{p^{k-1}-1} \, t^i} \pmod N,
   695	\]
   696	and then the same congruence holds modulo $A^p$.
   697	It follows that
   698	\begin{equation}
   699	\label{eqn:in p}
   700	\Big( \sum_{i=0}^{p^{k-1}-1} \, \zeta^i \Big) (\varphi(a)-\varphi(b)) \equiv 0 \pmod p
   701	\end{equation}
   702	in $R$.
   703	Now in the polynomial ring $\F_p[X]$ we have
   704	\[
   705	\sum_{i=0}^{p^{k-1}-1} \, X^i  = (X-1)^{p^{k-1}-1},
   706	\]
   707	and consequently
   708	\[
   709	\sum_{i=0}^{p^{k-1}-1} \, \zeta^i  \equiv (\zeta-1)^{p^{k-1}-1} \pmod{p}.
   710	\]
   711	If we replace this into (\ref{eqn:in p}) and use (\ref{eqn:p ideal}), we get
   712	\[
   713	\varphi(a)-\varphi(b) \in (\zeta-1)^{p^k-2p^{k-1}+1}.
   714	\]
   715	In particular, $\varphi(a)-\varphi(b)\in (\zeta-1)$ or, what is the same, $a\equiv b\pmod{P_k'}$.
   716	
   717	Now, by the previous paragraph, if $x,y\in\{t,ta_1,\ldots,ta_1^{p-1}\}$ and $x\ne y$, then $\langle xN \langle \cap \langle yN \rangle = 1$.
   718	Since $xN$ and $yN$ are generators of $P_k/N$ and $p\ge 5$, we conclude that $P_k/N$ is a Beauville group.
   719	\end{proof}
   720	
   721	
   722	
   723	\begin{cor}
   724	\label{cor:both implications fail}
   725	Let $p\ge 5$ be a prime.
   726	Then, for each of the implications in the criterion for Beauville groups given in Theorem A, there exist infinitely many $2$-generator $p$-groups (and even infinitely many $p$-groups of maximal class) for which the implication fails.
   727	\end{cor}
   728	
   729	
   730	
   731	\begin{proof}
   732	Consider arbitrary integers  $e>k\ge 1$, and let $N$ be a normal subgroup of $P_k$ such that $|A^{p^{e-1}}:N|=p$.
   733	By Theorem \ref{thm:quotients of Pk}, $G=P_k/N$ is a Beauville group.
   734	In the proof of that theorem, we have seen that all elements of the form $taN$ with $a\in A$ are of order $p^k$.
   735	It readily follows that every element of $P_k\smallsetminus A$ is of order at most $p^k$ when passing to $G$.
   736	Thus $\exp G=p^e$ and $G^{p^{e-1}}=A^{p^{e-1}}/N$ is of order $p$.
   737	This shows that the `only if' part of Theorem A fails for $G$.
   738	
   739	Let us construct a family of groups for which the `if' part fails.
   740	Take again $e>k\ge 1$, and consider now $N\trianglelefteq P_k$ lying between $A^{p^{e-1}}$ and $A^{p^e}$, and such that
   741	$|A^{p^{e-1}}:N|=p^m\ge p^2$.
   742	Let $L\trianglelefteq P_k$ be an intermediate subgroup between $N$ and $A^{p^{e-1}}$ such that $|L:N|=p$.
   743	Thus $L/N\le Z(P_k/N)$.
   744	Let us write $H=A/N$ and $Z=L/N$.
   745	By using the theory of cyclic extensions \cite[Section 3.7]{zas}, we can get a new group $G=\langle u,H \rangle$, where the action of $u$ on $H$ is again the one induced by multiplication by $\zeta$, but $u^{p^k}\in Z\smallsetminus 1$.
   746	Observe that $G$ is a $2$-generator group.
   747	A calculation as in (\ref{eqn:power of ta}) shows that every $x\in uH$ is now of order $p^{k+1}$, and $\langle x^{p^k} \rangle=Z$.
   748	Also, all elements of $G\smallsetminus H$ are of order at most $p^{k+1}$.
   749	Then $\exp G=p^e$ and $|G^{p^{e-1}}|=p^m$.
   750	Now, any set $\{x,y\}$ of generators of $G$ must contain an element, say $x$, outside the maximal subgroup $\langle u^p \rangle H$, and then a power of
   751	$x$ will be in $uH$.
   752	Consequently, $Z\subseteq \Sigma(x,y)$ and $G$ cannot be a Beauville group.
   753	\end{proof}
   754	
   755	
   756	
   757	We end this section by showing that it is not possible to find a variation of Theorem A which ensures the existence of Beauville structures in an arbitrary finite $p$-group, even if we strengthen the requirement on the size of $G^{p^{e-1}}$.
   758	Indeed, for every power of $p$ there are non-Beauville $p$-groups for which the order of $G^{p^{e-1}}$ is exactly that power.
   759	
   760	
   761	
   762	\begin{cor}
   763	For every prime $p\ge 5$, and positive integer $m$, there exists a $2$-generator $p$-group $G$ such that:
   764	\begin{enumerate}
   765	\item
   766	If $\exp G=p^e$ then $|G^{p^{e-1}}|=p^m$.
   767	\item
   768	$G$ is not a Beauville $p$-group.
   769	\end{enumerate}
   770	\end{cor}
   771	
   772	
   773	
   774	\begin{proof}
   775	Let $G=P_k/N$ be as in the second part of the proof of the last corollary.
   776	Since $\log_p |A^{p^{e-1}}:A^{p^e}| = p^{k-1}(p-1)$, we can make $|A^{p^{e-1}}:N|$ as large as we want by taking $k$ big enough.
   777	This gives the desired groups.
   778	\end{proof}
