   781	
   782	\section{Quotients of the Nottingham group over $\F_p$ for odd $p$}
   783	\label{nott}
   784	
   785	
   786	
   787	In this section, given an odd prime $p$, we determine which (finite) quotients of the Nottingham group $\No$ over $\F_p$ are Beauville groups.
   788	One of the consequences of our study will be that, in the case $p=3$, there are quotients of $\No$ which are Beauville groups of order $3^n$,
   789	for every $n\ge 5$.
   790	This provides the first explicit infinite family of Beauville $3$-groups in the literature.
   791	
   792	Given a fixed positive integer $k$, the automorphisms $f\in\No$ such that $f(t)=t+\sum_{i\ge k+1} \, a_it^i$ form
   793	an open normal subgroup $\No_k$ of $\No$.
   794	Observe that $|\No_k:\No_{k+1}|=p$ for all $k\ge 1$, and that the condition $f\equiv g \pmod{\No_k}$ in the group
   795	$\No$ is equivalent to the congruence $f(t)\equiv g(t) \pmod{t^{k+1}}$ in the ring $\F_p[[t]]$.
   796	By Theorem 1.3 of \cite{klo2}, every non-trivial abstract (i.e.\ not necessarily closed) normal subgroup $\WW$ of $\No$ satisfies either
   797	$\No_{k+1}\le \WW \le \No_k$ for some $k\ge 1$ or $\No_{k+2}\le \WW \le \No_k$ for some $k\equiv 1\pmod p$.
   798	In particular, $\WW$ is of finite index in $\No$.
   799	
   800	We recall the following formulas for $p$th powers and commutators of the subgroups $\No_k$
   801	(see \cite{cam}, Theorem 6 and Theorem 2, respectively.)
   802	In the case of powers, we have
   803	\begin{equation}
   804	\label{powers Ni}
   805	\No_k^p = \No_{pk+r},
   806	\quad
   807	\text{where $0\le r\le p-1$ is the residue of $k$ modulo $p$.}
   808	\end{equation}
   809	On the other hand, we have the commutator formula
   810	\begin{equation}
   811	\label{comms Ni}
   812	[\No_k,\No_{\ell}]
   813	=
   814	\begin{cases}
   815	\No_{k+\ell},
   816	&
   817	\text{if $k\not\equiv \ell\pmod p$,}
   818	\\
   819	\No_{k+\ell+1},
   820	&
   821	\text{if $k\equiv \ell\pmod p$.}
   822	\end{cases}
   823	\end{equation}
   824	It follows that $\No'=\No_3$ and, more generally, $\gamma_i(\No)=\No_{s(i)}$, where $s(i)=i+1+\lfloor (i-2)/(p-1) \rfloor$.
   825	As a consequence, $|\gamma_i(\No):\gamma_{i+1}(\No)|\le p^2$, and we have `diamonds' of order $p^2$ if and only if $i$ is of the form $i=k(p-1)+1$ for some $k\ge 0$.
   826	In other words, the diamonds in the lower central series of $\No$ correspond to quotients $\No_{kp+1}/\No_{kp+3}$.
   827	Another consequence of (\ref{comms Ni}) is the following: if
   828	$f\in\No_k\smallsetminus \No_{k+1}$ and $g\in\No_{\ell}\smallsetminus \No_{\ell+1}$ with $k\not\equiv\ell \pmod p$, then
   829	$[f,g]\in \No_{k+\ell}\smallsetminus \No_{k+\ell+1}$.
   830	
   831	
   832	
   833	On the other hand, every non-trivial normal subgroup $\WW$ of $\No$ is either a term of the series $\{\No_k\}$ or it is an intermediate subgroup in a diamond, i.e. we have $\No_{kp+3}<\WW<\No_{kp+1}$ for some $k\ge 0$.
   834	In the latter case, there are $p+1$ subgroups for every $k$; if $e$ and $f$ are the automorphisms defined via
   835	$e(t)=t+t^{kp+2}$ and $f=t+t^{kp+3}$, then these subgroups are $\langle ef^i , \No_{kp+3} \rangle$, where $i=0,\ldots,p-1$, together with
   836	$\No_{kp+2}=\langle f , \No_{kp+3} \rangle$.
   837	
   838	In the following, we write $z_m$ for the number $p^m+p^{m-1}+\cdots+p+2$, for every $m\ge 1$.
   839	We extend this definition to the case $m=0$ by putting $z_0=2$.
   840	Observe that $\No_{z_m-1}/\No_{z_m+1}$ is a diamond for all $m\ge 0$; we refer to these as
   841	\emph{distinguished diamonds\/}.
   842	By (\ref{powers Ni}), we have
   843	\begin{equation}
   844	\label{powers Nzm}
   845	\No_{z_m+1}^{p^n} \subseteq \No_{z_{m+n}+1},
   846	\quad
   847	\text{for all $m,n\ge 0$.}
   848	\end{equation}
   849	
   850	
   851	
   852	Our approach to the determination of Beauville structures in quotients of the Nottingham group is based on the analysis of the specific quotients of the form $\No/\No_{z_m+1}$, i.e.\ when we factor out $\No$ at the bottom of a distinguished diamond.
   853	To this purpose, it is fundamental to control the $p^m$th powers of elements outside $\No'$ (which are potential generators) in the factor group $\No/\No_{z_m+1}$.
   854	We need some lemmas.
   855	
   856	
   857	
   858	\begin{lem}
   859	\label{all_elements}
   860	Let $f\in\No_{z_k-1}$ and $g \in \No_{z_k+1}$, where $k\ge 0$.
   861	Then, for every $\ell\ge 1$ we have
   862	\[
   863	(fg)^{p^{\ell}} \equiv f^{p^{\ell}} \pmod{ \No_{z_{k+\ell}+1}}.
   864	\]
   865	\end{lem}
   866	
   867	
   868	
   869	\begin{proof}
   870	By the Hall-Petrescu formula (see \cite[III.9.4]{hup} or \cite[page 37]{suz2}), we have
   871	\begin{equation}
   872	\label{hall-petrescu}
   873	(fg)^{p^{\ell}}= f^{p^{\ell}}g^{p^{\ell}}c_2^{\binom{p^{\ell}}{2}}c_3^{\binom{p^{\ell}}{3}} \dots c_{p-1}^{\binom{p^{\ell}}{p-1}} \dots c_{p^{\ell}},
   874	\end{equation}
   875	where $c_i \in \gamma_i(\langle f,g \rangle)$.
   876	Let $1\le i\le p^{\ell}$, and choose $r$ such that $p^r\le i<p^{r+1}$.
   877	Then the binomial coefficient $\binom{p^{\ell}}{i}$ is divisible by $p^{\ell-r}$, by Kummer's Theorem.
   878	Also,
   879	\[
   880	c_i \in \gamma_{p^r}(\langle f,g \rangle)
   881	\le 
   882	[\No_{z_k+1}, \No_{z_k-1}, \overset{p^r-1}{\ldots},\No_{z_k-1}]
   883	=
   884	\No_{2+p^r(z_k-1)+\frac{p^r-1}{p-1}},
   885	\]
   886	by using (\ref{comms Ni}).
   887	Now, since
   888	\[
   889	2+p^r(z_k-1)+\frac{p^r-1}{p-1} = z_{r+k}+1,
   890	\]
   891	we get
   892	\[
   893	c_i ^{\binom{p^{\ell}}{i}} \in \No_{z_{r+k}+1}^{p^{\ell-r}} \le \No_{p^{\ell-r}(z_{r+k}+1)} \le \No_{z_{k+\ell}+1},
   894	\]
   895	by (\ref{powers Nzm}).
   896	On the other hand,
   897	\[
   898	g^{p^{\ell}} \in \No_{z_k+1}^{p^{\ell}} \leq \No_{p^{\ell}(z_k+1)} \leq \No_{z_{k+\ell}+1},
   899	\]
   900	and we conclude from (\ref{hall-petrescu}) that $(fg)^{p^{\ell}} \equiv f^{p^{\ell}} \pmod{ \No_{z_{k+\ell}+1}}$.

