   535	In \cite[Conjecture 25]{BBF}, the authors conjecture that the two non-isomorphic groups of order $p^5$ under the names $H_3$ and $H_4$ in that paper are Beauville for $p\ge 5$ and, as a consequence, that there are exactly $p+10$ Beauville groups of order $p^5$ for $p\ge 5$.
   536	This can be confirmed in a straightforward way by using Proposition \ref{easy detecting}.
   537	Indeed, both isomorphism types can be described in the form
   538	\begin{gather*}
   539	G = \langle a, b, c \mid a^{p^2}=b^{p^2}=c^p=[b,c]=1,\ [a,b]=c,\ [a,c]=b^{rp} \rangle,
   540	\end{gather*}
   541	where $r$ is not divisible by $p$.
   542	Observe that $G=\langle a,b \rangle$.
   543	Since $\exp G=p^2$ and $\langle a^p \rangle \ne \langle b^p \rangle$ are non-trivial, we conclude that $G$ is a Beauville group.
   544	\end{exa}
