   906	Thus if we want to know the $p^m$th powers in $\No/\No_{z_m+1}$ corresponding to all elements outside $\No'$,
   907	it suffices to calculate that power for one specific element in every difference $\MM\smallsetminus \No'$, as $\MM$ runs over the $p+1$ maximal subgroups of $\No$.
   908	These maximal subgroups are $\No_2$ and the subgroups $\MM_{\lambda}=\langle f_{\lambda} \rangle \No'$ for
   909	all $\lambda\in\F_p$, where $f_{\lambda}$ is given by $f_{\lambda}(t)= t+t^2+ \lambda t^3$.
   910	By \cite[Proposition 1.2]{klo}, the elements $a\in \MM_1\smallsetminus \No'$ and
   911	$b\in \No_2\smallsetminus \No'$ given by $a(t)=t(1-t)^{-1}$ and $b(t)=t(1-2t)^{-1/2}$ are both of order $p$.
   912	Thus all elements in $\MM_1\smallsetminus \No'$ and $\No_2\smallsetminus \No'$ have order at most $p^m$ in the quotient $\No/\No_{z_m+1}$.
   913	Our next goal is to see that the situation is completely different in the maximal subgroups
   914	$\MM_{\lambda}$ with $\lambda\ne 1$, and for that we need to know $f_{\lambda}^{p^m}$ modulo
   915	$\No_{z_m+1}$.
