   919	Before we proceed, let us mention how one can calculate $p^m$th powers of elements in the Nottingham group.
   920	Given $f\in\No$, we can form a unitriangular matrix $M$ with infinitely many rows and columns, by letting $M_{i,j}$ be the coefficient of $t^j$ in the power series $f(t^i)$, the image of $t^i$ under $f$.
   921	Then we have the following result \cite[Lemma 5]{yor}.
   922	
   923	
   924	
   925	\begin{lem}
   926	Let $f\in\No$, and let $M$ be the matrix associated to $f$.
   927	Then, for every $r\ge 1$, the coefficient of $t^n$ in the series $f^{p^r}(t)$ is
   928	\begin{equation}
   929	\label{coefficient fpm}
   930	\sum_{\mathbf{i}=(i_0,\ldots,i_{\ell})} \, M_{i_0,i_1} M_{i_1,i_2} \ldots M_{i_{\ell-1},i_{\ell}},
   931	\end{equation}
   932	where $\ell=p^r$ and the tuples $\mathbf{i}=(i_0,\ldots,i_{\ell})$ in the sum are taken so that
   933	$1=i_0<i_1<i_2<\cdots<i_{\ell-1}<i_{\ell}=n$.
   934	\end{lem}
