  1159	We can now begin to determine which quotients of the Nottingham group are Beauville groups.
  1160	Firstly, we consider quotients of the form $\No/\No_k$.
  1161	We deal separately with the cases $p>3$ and $p=3$.
  1162	
  1163	
  1164	
  1165	\begin{thm}
  1166	\label{nott p>3}
  1167	If $p\ge 5$ then a quotient $\No/\No_k$ is a Beauville group if and only if $k\ge 3$ and $k\ne z_m$ for all $m\ge 1$. 
  1168	\end{thm}
  1169	
  1170	
  1171	
  1172	\begin{proof}
  1173	First of all, we show that $G=\No/\No_{z_m+1}$ is a Beauville group for all $m\ge 1$.
  1174	Let $u$ and $v$ be the images in $G$ of the automorphisms $a$ and $b$ which were defined after Lemma \ref{all_elements}.
  1175	Then $\{u,v\}$ and $\{uv^2,uv^4\}$ are both systems of generators of $G$, and we claim that they yield a Beauville structure for $G$.
  1176	If $X=\{u,v,uv\}$ and $Y=\{uv^2,uv^4,uv^2uv^4\}$, we have to see that
  1177	\begin{equation}
  1178	\label{check}
  1179	\langle x^g \rangle \cap \langle y^h \rangle=1
  1180	\end{equation}
  1181	for all $x\in X$, $y\in Y$, and $g,h\in G$.
  1182	Observe that $\langle x\Phi(G) \rangle$ and $\langle y\Phi(G) \rangle$ have trivial intersection for every $x\in X$ and
  1183	$y\in Y$, since $a$ and $b$ are linearly independent modulo $\Phi(G)$.
  1184	As a consequence, $x^g$ and $y^h$ lie in different maximal subgroups of $G$ in every case.
  1185	
  1186	Assume first that $x=u$ or $v$, which are elements of order $p$.
  1187	If (\ref{check}) does not hold, then $\langle x^g \rangle \subseteq \langle y^h \rangle$, and consequently $\langle x\Phi(G) \rangle=\langle y\Phi(G) \rangle$, which is a contradiction.
  1188	Thus we assume that $x=uv$.
  1189	Now, $uv$ and all elements $y\in Y$ lie in $M_{\lambda}\smallsetminus G'$ for some
  1190	$\lambda\in\F_p$, $\lambda\ne 1$, and so they are all of order $p^{m+1}$, by Corollary \ref{pmth powers}.
  1191	If (\ref{check}) does not hold, then
  1192	\[
  1193	\langle (x^g)^{p^m} \rangle = \langle (y^h)^{p^m} \rangle
  1194	\]
  1195	and, again by Corollary \ref{pmth powers}, $x^g,y^h\in M_{\lambda}$ for some
  1196	$\lambda$.
  1197	This is a contradiction, and we thus complete the proof that $G$ is a Beauville group.
  1198	
  1199	Let us now consider a quotient $\No/\No_k$ with $k\ge 3$ and $k\ne z_m$ for all
  1200	$m\ge 1$.
  1201	Then either $3\le k\le p+1$ or $z_m+1\le k\le z_{m+1}-1$ for some $m\ge 1$.
  1202	In the former case, $\No/\No_3\cong C_p\times C_p$ is a Beauville group, since
  1203	$p\ge 5$, and $\exp \No/\No_k=\exp \No/\No_3=p$.
  1204	Thus $\No/\No_k$ is a Beauville group by Lemma \ref{lifting structure}.
  1205	In the latter case, $\No/\No_{z_m+1}$ has the Beauville structure shown in the previous paragraph, whose first set of generators is $\{a\No_{z_m+1},b\No_{z_m+1}\}$.
  1206	Since $o(a \No_k)=o(b \No_k)=p$ and $o(ab \No_k)=p^{m+1}$, since
  1207	$(ab)^{p^{m+1}}\in \No_{z_{m+1}-1}$ by Corollary \ref{pmth powers}, we can again apply Lemma \ref{lifting structure}, and $\No/\No_k$ is a Beauville group also in this case.
