  1236	
  1237	\begin{proof}
  1238	We assume that $h= (x^i)^{g_1}=((xt)^j)^{g_2}$ for some $i,j \in \mathbb{Z}$ and $g_1, g_2 \in G$, and prove that
  1239	$h=1$.
  1240	Since $t\in\Phi(G)$, we have $x^i\Phi(G)=x^j\Phi(G)$, and so $i\equiv j \pmod{p}$.
  1241	Then $h=(x^{g_1})^j=((xt)^{g_2})^j$, since $x$ is of order $p$.
  1242	If $p\mid j$ then we are done.
  1243	If $p\nmid j$ then, since $G$ is a $p$-group, $x^{g_1}=(xt)^{g_2}$, and consequently $t= [x,g_1{g_2}^{-1} ]$, which is a contradiction.
