  1271	\[
  1272	\{[u,g]\mid g\in G\} \cap N_{z_m}=1.
  1273	\]
  1274	To see this, assume that $[u,g]\in N_{z_m}$.
  1275	Since $a\in\No_1\smallsetminus \No_2$ is of order $p$, (\ref{centralizer}) yields
  1276	\[
  1277	C_{\No/\No_{z_m}}(a\No_{z_m}) = C_{\No}(a)\No_{z_m-1}/\No_{z_m}.
  1278	\]
  1279	Thus we can write $g=ch$, with $[u,c]=1$ and $h\in N_{z_m-1}$.
  1280	It follows that $[u,g]=[u,h]\in [G,N_{z_m-1}]=1$, since
  1281	$N_{z_m-1}$ is central in $G$ (it corresponds to the diamond 
  1282	$\No_{z_m-1}/\No_{z_m+1}$ in $\No$).
  1283	
  1284	We prove the result for the element $v$ by showing that, whenever
  1285	$[v,g]\in N_{z_m-1}$, we actually have $[v,g]\in N_{z_m}$.
  1286	Since $b\in\No_2\smallsetminus \No_3$ is of order $p$, we have
  1287	\[
  1288	C_{\No/\No_{z_m-2}}(b\No_{z_m-2}) = C_{\No}(b)\No_{z_m-4}/\No_{z_m-2}.
  1289	\]
  1290	Thus we can write $g=ch$ with $[v,c]=1$ and $h\in N_{z_m-4}$, and consequently
  1291	$[v,g]=[v,h]$.
  1292	Now, since $[N_2,N_{z_m-3}]=N_{z_m}$ and the commutator of 
  1293	$v\in N_2\smallsetminus N_3$ with an element of
  1294	$N_{z_m-4}\smallsetminus N_{z_m-3}$ lies in $N_{z_m-2}\smallsetminus N_{z_m-1}$,
  1295	we conclude that $[v,g]\in N_{z_m}$, as desired.
  1296	\end{proof}
  1297	
  1298	
  1299	
  1300	\begin{thm}
  1301	\label{nott p=3}
  1302	If $p=3$ then a quotient $\No/\No_k$ is a Beauville group if and only if $k\ge 6$ and
  1303	$k\ne z_m$ for all $m\ge 1$. 
  1304	\end{thm}
  1305	
  1306	
  1307	
  1308	\begin{proof}
  1309	Since the smallest order of a Beauville $3$-group is $3^5$, the quotient $\No/\No_k$ can only be a Beauville $3$-group if
  1310	$k\ge 6$; note that $6$ is the same as $z_1+1$ in this case.
  1311	Now, by arguing as in the proof of Theorem \ref{nott p>3}, it suffices to see that
  1312	$G=\No/\No_{z_m+1}$ is a Beauville group for every $m\ge 1$.
  1313	Recall that $N_k=\No_k/\No_{z_m+1}$ and $M_{\lambda}=\MM_{\lambda}/\No_{z_m+1}$.
  1314	
  1315	Let $u$ and $v$ be the images of $a$ and $b$ in $G$, respectively.
  1316	By Lemma \ref{not covered}, there exist $w,z\in N_{z_m-1}$ such that
  1317	$w\not\in \{[u,g]\mid g\in G\}$ and $z\not\in \{[v,g]\mid g\in G\}$.
  1318	Observe that $w$ and $z$ are central elements of order $p$ in $G$.
  1319	
  1320	We claim that $\{u,v\}$ and $\{(uw)^{-1},vz\}$ form a Beauville structure in $G$.
  1321	Let $X=\{u,v,uv\}$ and $Y=\{(uw)^{-1},vz,u^{-1}vw^{-1}z\}$.
  1322	Assume first that $x\in X$ is of order $p$, and let $y\in Y$.
  1323	If $\langle x\Phi(G) \rangle \ne \langle y\Phi(G) \rangle$ in $G/\Phi(G)$, then we get
  1324	$\langle x\rangle^g \cap \langle y \rangle^h=1$ for every $g,h\in G$, as in the proof of Theorem \ref{nott p>3}.
  1325	Otherwise, we are in one of the following two cases: $x=u$ and $y=(uw)^{-1}$, or
  1326	$x=v$ and $y=vz$.
  1327	Then the condition $\langle x \rangle^g \cap \langle y \rangle^h=1$ follows by combining Lemma \ref {intersection_2} and Lemma \ref{not covered}.
  1328	
  1329	Since the same argument applies when $y\in Y$ is of order $p$, we are only left with the case when $x=uv$ and $y=u^{-1}vw^{-1}z$.
  1330	Now $x$ and $y$ lie in two different maximal subgroups, which are also different from $N_2$ and $M_1$.
  1331	By Corollary \ref{pmth powers}, both $x$ and $y$ are of order $p^{m+1}$ and
  1332	$\langle x^{p^m} \rangle \ne \langle y^{p^m} \rangle$.
  1333	Since $x^{p^m},y^{p^m}\in N_{z_m-1}$ are central in $G$, it follows that
  1334	$\langle x \rangle^g \cap \langle y \rangle^h=1$ for all $g,h\in G$ also in this case.
  1335	This completes the proof.
  1336	\end{proof}
  1337	
  1338	
  1339	
  1340	Finally, we analyse the quotients of the form $\No/\WW$, where $\WW$ is an intermediate subgroup in a diamond
  1341	$\No_{kp+1}/\No_{kp+3}$, i.e. $\No_{kp+3}<\WW<\No_{kp+1}$.
  1342	
  1343	
  1344	
  1345	\begin{thm}
  1346	\label{thm:quotient by intermediate}
  1347	Let $\WW$ be an intermediate subgroup in a diamond of the Nottingham group.
  1348	Then:
  1349	\begin{enumerate}
  1350	\item
  1351	If the diamond that contains $\WW$ is not distinguished, then $\No/\WW$ is a Beauville group.
  1352	\item
  1353	If the diamond that contains $\WW$ is distinguished, say $\No_{z_m+1}<\WW<\No_{z_m-1}$, then $\No/\WW$ is a Beauville group if and only if $m\ge 1$ or $m\ge 2$, according as $p>3$ or $p=3$, and furthermore $\WW \ne \No_{z_m}, \langle e,\No_{z_m+1} \rangle$, where $e$ is the automorphism given by $e(t)=t+t^{z_m}$.
  1354	\end{enumerate}
  1355	\end{thm}
  1356	
  1357	
  1358	
  1359	\begin{proof}
  1360	(i)
  1361	Let $\No_{kp+3}<\WW<\No_{kp+1}$, and choose $m$ as large as possible such that $z_m+1<kp+3$.
  1362	By Theorems \ref{nott p>3} and \ref{nott p=3}, we have a Beauville structure in $\No/\No_{z_m+1}$ in which one of the sets of generators is
  1363	$\{a\No_{z_m+1},b\No_{z_m+1}\}$.
  1364	Now one can readily check that $a$, $b$ and $ab$ have the same order modulo $\WW$ and modulo $\No_{z_m+1}$, namely $p$, $p$ and $p^{m+1}$.
  1365	Hence $\No/\WW$ is a Beauville group by Lemma \ref{lifting structure}.
  1366	
  1367	(ii)
  1368	By looking at the order of $\No/\WW$, it is clear that the conditions $m\ge 1$ if $p>3$ and $m\ge 2$ if $p=3$ are necessary for $\No/\WW$ to be a Beauville group.
  1369	On the other hand, the $p+1$ intermediate subgroups between $\No_{z_m+1}$ and $\No_{z_m-1}$ are $\No_{z_m}$ and the subgroups
  1370	$\WW_{\alpha}=\langle e_{\alpha}, \No_{z_m+1} \rangle$, where $\alpha\in\F_p$ and $e_{\alpha}(t)=t+t^{z_m}+\alpha t^{z_m+1}$.
  1371	We already know that $\No/\No_{z_m}$ is not a Beauville group, and the same argument shows that neither $\No/\WW_0$ is a Beauville group.
  1372	Thus it suffices to consider the case where $\WW=\WW_{\alpha}$ for some $\alpha\ne 0$, and prove that $\No/\WW$ is a Beauville group in that case.
  1373	If we define $f_{\lambda}$ as above by means of $f_{\lambda}(t)=t+t^2+\lambda t^3$, then Corollary \ref{p^mth_powers} yields that
  1374	\[
  1375	f_{1+\alpha}^{p^m} \equiv e_{\alpha}^{(-\alpha)^m}  \pmod{\No_{z_m+1}},
  1376	\]
  1377	and consequently $\WW=\langle f_{1+\alpha}^{p^m}, \No_{z_m+1} \rangle$.
  1378	Now observe that $f_{1+\alpha}\equiv ab^{\alpha} \pmod{\No_3}$ which, according to Lemma \ref{all_elements}, implies that
  1379	\[
  1380	f_{1+\alpha}^{p^m}\equiv (ab^{\alpha})^{p^m} \pmod{\No_{z_m+1}}.
  1381	\]
  1382	Hence $\WW=\langle (ab^{\alpha})^{p^m}, \No_{z_m+1} \rangle$.
  1383	In particular, the order of $ab^{\alpha}$ modulo $\WW$ is $p^m$.
  1384	
  1385	Now, since $m\ge 1$ if $p>3$ and $m\ge 2$ if $p=3$, $\No/\No_{z_{m-1}+1}$ has a Beauville structure with $\{a\No_{z_{m-1}+1},b\No_{z_{m-1}+1}\}$ as one of the generating sets.
  1386	One can similarly see that there is a Beauville structure with $\{a\No_{z_{m-1}+1},b^{\alpha}\No_{z_{m-1}+1}\}$.
  1387	Since $o(a\No_{z_{m-1}+1})=o(a\WW)=p$, $o(b\No_{z_{m-1}+1})=o(b\WW)=p$ and
  1388	$o(ab^{\alpha}\No_{z_{m-1}+1})=o(ab^{\alpha}\WW)=p^m$, we can apply Lemma \ref{lifting structure} to conclude that
  1389	$\No/\WW$ has a Beauville structure, as desired.
  1390	\end{proof}
  1391	
  1392	
  1393	
  1394	At this point, Theorem B is straightforward.
  1395	
  1396	
  1397	
  1398	\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorem B]
  1399	Put $n_0=2$ or $5$ according as $p>3$ or $p=3$, as in the statement of Theorem B.
  1400	Then Theorems \ref{nott p>3} and \ref{nott p=3} provide Beauville groups which are quotients of the Nottingham group of order $p^n$ for every $n\ge n_0$, with the only exception of the values $n=p^m+\cdots+p+1$, where $m\ge 1$ if $p>3$ and $m\ge 2$ if $p=3$.
  1400	Then Theorems \ref{nott p>3} and \ref{nott p=3} provide Beauville groups which are quotients of the Nottingham group of order $p^n$ for every $n\ge n_0$, with the only exception of the values $n=p^m+\cdots+p+1$, where $m\ge 1$ if $p>3$ and $m\ge 2$ if $p=3$.
  1401	Now, according to the previous theorem, the missing orders can be obtained by factoring $\No$ with an adequate intermediate subgroup in the distinguished diamond $\No_{z_m-1}/\No_{z_m+1}$.
  1402	\end{proof}
  1403	
  1404	
  1405	
  1406	We close the paper by showing that condition (i) in Proposition \ref{negative} cannot be relaxed.
  1407	More specifically, given a $2$-generator finite $p$-group $G$ of exponent $p^e$ in which $\Omega_{\{e-1\}}(G)$ is contained in the union of three maximal subgroups and
  1408	$|G^{p^{e-1}}|=p$, it may well happen that $G$ is a Beauville group.
  1409	To this end, consider an intermediate subgroup $\No_{z_m+1}<\WW<\No_{z_m-1}$ in a distinguished diamond of the Nottingham group, where $m\ge 1$ or $m\ge 2$, according as $p>3$ or $p=3$.
  1410	If $\WW \ne \No_{z_m}, \langle e,\No_{z_m+1} \rangle$ then $G=\No/\WW$ is a Beauville group by Theorem \ref{thm:quotient by intermediate}.
  1411	Also, as indicated in the proof of that theorem, we have $\WW=\langle (ab^{\alpha})^{p^m},\No_{z_m+1} \rangle$ for some $\alpha\ne 0$ in $\F_p$.
  1412	It then follows from Corollary \ref{pmth powers} that $\exp G=p^{m+1}$ and that $\Omega_{\{m\}}(G)$ is contained in the three maximal subgroups of $G$ that contain the images of $a$, $b$ and $ab^{\alpha}$.
