  1063	The coefficient $\beta$ of $t^{z_m+1}$ in $f^p(t)$ can be obtained in a similar way.
  1064	Again, $\beta$ is a product of factors of the form $M_{i_{j-1},i_j}$, where $i_j-i_{j-1}=z_{m-1}-1$ except for two values
  1065	$k$ and $\ell$ for which $i_k-i_{k-1}=i_{\ell}-i_{\ell-1}=z_{m-1}$, or one value $r$ for which
  1066	$i_r-i_{r-1}=z_{m-1}+1$.
  1067	The latter case gives a zero product, since $M_{i,i+z_{m-1}+1}=0$ by (\ref{M for m>1}), and consequently
  1068	\[
  1069	\beta
  1070	=
  1071	\sum_{1\le k<\ell \le p}
  1072	\, \beta_{k,\ell},
  1073	\]
  1074	where
  1075	\begin{multline*}
  1076	\beta_{k,l}
  1077	=
  1078	\Big( \prod_{i=1}^{k-1} M_{q_{i-1},q_i} \Big)
  1079	\,
  1080	M_{q_{k-1},q_k+1}
  1081	\,
  1082	\Big( \hspace{-2pt} \prod_{i=k+1}^{\ell-1} M_{q_{i-1}+1,q_i+1} \Big)
  1083	\\
  1084	M_{q_{\ell-1}+1,q_\ell+2}
  1085	\,
  1086	\Big( \hspace{-2pt} \prod_{i=\ell+1}^p M_{q_{i-1}+2,q_i+2} \Big).
  1087	\end{multline*}
  1088	By (\ref{M for m>1}), we have
  1089	\[
  1090	\beta_{k,\ell} = \lambda^{p-2}\mu^2 \prod_{\substack{i=1\\ i\ne k+1,\,\ell+2}}^{p+2} \, i,
  1091	\]
  1092	which is $0$ unless $k=p-1$ and $\ell=p$, or $1\le k\le p-3$ and $\ell=p-2$.
  1093	Consequently
  1094	\[
  1095	\beta
  1096	=
  1097	\lambda^{p-2}\mu^2 \Big( (p-1)! (p+1) + \sum_{k=1}^{p-3} \, \frac{(p-1)! (p+1)(p+2)}{k+1} \Big)
  1098	=
  1099	-\lambda^{p-2}\mu^2,
  1100	\]
  1101	where the last equality follows from the fact that
  1102	\[
  1103	\sum_{i=1}^{p-1} \, \frac{(p-1)!}{i} \equiv 0 \pmod p
  1104	\]
  1105	for odd $p$, since it coincides with the sum of the inverses of all elements of $\F_p^{\times}$.
  1106	This completes the proof when $m>1$.
  1107	
  1108	Finally, the case $m=1$ can be dealt with in a similar way, and the details are left to the reader.
  1109	\end{proof}
