The paper does expose a viable Track B family, but not the initially suggested “find a Beauville pair”: in the relevant 2-generator quotients, valid pairs have constant-density images in the Frattini quotient, so random witnesses are too easy. I’m instead using the paper’s native Nottingham-group power-series object: compute the sparse leading part of a huge \(p^m\)-fold iterate. Section 3 gives both the mechanical coefficient-sum route and the compact recurrence, which cleanly separates no-tool discovery from exact verification.
